<section class="panel" aria-label="First-party 2021 Grade 3 fractions and whole numbers">
<h2>Original mental number talk: divide by four</h2><p>12÷4=3; 24÷4=6; 60÷4=15; 72÷4=18. Explain your division strategy.</p>
<h2>Original three lists — 31 fractions</h2><p>In the source, each member of a <strong>three-student group</strong> takes one complete list and explains number-line placements. Each equal interval is one half, one third or one fourth of the same 0-to-1 whole.</p>
<table class="source-chart"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Assigned group</th><th scope="col">Complete source fraction list</th><th scope="col">Those at whole-number positions</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">10 halves</th><td>1/2, 2/2, 3/2, 4/2, 5/2, 6/2, 7/2, 8/2, 9/2, 10/2</td><td>2/2=1; 4/2=2; 6/2=3; 8/2=4; 10/2=5</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">9 thirds</th><td>1/3, 2/3, 3/3, 4/3, 5/3, 6/3, 7/3, 8/3, 9/3</td><td>3/3=1; 6/3=2; 9/3=3</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">12 fourths</th><td>1/4, 2/4, 3/4, 4/4, 5/4, 6/4, 7/4, 8/4, 9/4, 10/4, 11/4, 12/4</td><td>4/4=1; 8/4=2; 12/4=3</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>There are <strong>11</strong> original fractional terms naming whole numbers, out of <strong>31</strong> labels total. Counting exact source terms is not the same as grading the physical three-student conversation.</p>
<h2>Activity 2: find 1 from a given unit fraction</h2><p>On each original blank line, zero and the named unit fraction are provided. Students must <strong>construct where 1 is</strong> by repeating that unit's spacing; then explain how to locate 2.</p>
<table class="source-chart"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Known unit fraction</th><th scope="col">Steps to 1</th><th scope="col">Steps to 2</th><th scope="col">Fraction labels for whole numbers</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">1/2</th><td>2</td><td>4</td><td>1=2/2; 2=4/2</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">1/4</th><td>4</td><td>8</td><td>1=4/4; 2=8/4</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">1/6</th><td>6</td><td>12</td><td>1=6/6; 2=12/6</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">1/8</th><td>8</td><td>16</td><td>1=8/8; 2=16/8</td></tr>
</tbody></table><p>Mark one unit-fraction-length jump repeatedly with equal spacing. Teacher reviews spacing and how each pupil derived whole-number positions.</p>
